{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And I bore you on eagles' wings and I brought you to Me.</b> He mentioned here three levels, one above the other. First He said, 'וָאֶשָּׂא אֶתְכֶם עַל כַּנְפֵי נְשָׁרִים' — and this is as when a nurse carries a suckling child, and then the Holy One, blessed be He, is at the level of a father and Israel at the level of a son. After that He said, 'וָאָבִא אֶתְכֶם אֵלָי' — implying that you shall be equal to Me and we shall be like two brothers equal in their level. And after that He said, 'וְאַתֶּם תִּהְיוּ לִי מַמְלֶכֶת' — as it were, kings to Me, as it is written (II Samuel 23:3) 'צַדִּיק מוֹשֵׁל יִרְאַת ה׳'. And this is explained by way of Midrash Ḥazita, which the Ramban brought in parashat Ḥayyei Sarah (Genesis 24:1), and this is its language: Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai asked Rabbi Elazar bar Yosei: Is it possible that you heard from your father what is meant by 'בָּעֲטָרָה שֶׁעִטְּרָה לּוֹ אִמּוֹ' (Song of Songs 3:11)? He said to him: A parable of a king who had an only daughter and cherished her exceedingly. At first he called her 'my daughter.' He did not cease cherishing her until he called her 'my sister.' He did not cease cherishing her until he called her 'my mother.' So too the Holy One, blessed be He, cherishes Israel and called them 'my daughter,' as it is said (Psalms 45:11) 'שִׁמְעִי בַת וּרְאִי.' He did not cease cherishing them until he called them 'my sister,' as it is said (Song of Songs 5:2) 'פִּתְחִי לִי אֲחֹתִי רַעְיָתִי.' He did not cease cherishing them until he called them 'my mother,' as it is said (Isaiah 51:4) 'הַקְשִׁיבוּ אֵלַי עַמִּי וּלְאֻמִּי' — 'לְאִמִּי' is how it is written." "And as it appears, this midrash did not state the order of the levels in sequence, for first comes 'daughter,' and afterward he added to his cherishing and called her 'my sister' — and this addition is a diminution, for without doubt a person cherishes his daughter more than his sister. And without doubt this midrash speaks of the levels of dominion that one has over the other: for a father rules over his daughter, and likewise a mother, but a brother and sister are equal. So at first the Holy One, blessed be He, called Israel 'daughter,' from the side of His love for Israel, but to Him, blessed be He, belongs the kingship to rule over Israel like the dominion of a father over his daughter. And this was in the days of Abraham, for the verse 'שִׁמְעִי בַת וּרְאִי' (Psalms 45:11) was interpreted concerning Abraham (Bereshit Rabbah 39:1), and this is the level spoken of here first: 'וָאֶשָּׂא אֶתְכֶם עַל כַּנְפֵי נְשָׁרִים' — and this bearing is as when a nurse carries a suckling child. Afterward the Holy One, blessed be He, raised them to a level greater than this, to call them 'my sister' — to say that they are equal to Him, blessed be He, and the Holy One, blessed be He, equalized His honor and His awe, blessed be He, to their honor. And as the Sages, of blessed memory, said: the righteous resemble partners with the Holy One, blessed be He. And this was in the days of Isaac, for the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him, 'גּוּר בָּאָרֶץ הַזֹּאת וְאֶהְיֶה עִמָּךָ' (Genesis 26:3) — that is, on equal terms with you, for his merit was added upon the merit of Abraham his father; therefore the expression 'וְאֶהְיֶה עִמָּךָ' was not said to Abraham as it was to Isaac. And this is the second level spoken of here: 'וָאָבִא אֶתְכֶם אֵלָי' — for this resembles the statement 'וְאֶהְיֶה עִמָּךָ.' Afterward, when Jacob came — the portion of His inheritance, the threefold cord — HaShem raised him to a level greater than this, to call him 'mother': just as a mother rules over her daughter, so does a righteous one rule in the fear of HaShem, as the Sages, of blessed memory, interpreted (Mo'ed Katan 16a–b). And this is what is said of Jacob: 'כִּי שָׂרִיתָ עִם אֱלֹהִים וְעִם אֲנָשִׁים וַתּוּכָל' (Genesis 32:29). Even though it was also said to Jacob (Genesis 31:3) 'וְאֶהְיֶה עִמָּךְ,' nevertheless afterward he was raised to a third level, when he strove with El and prevailed. And this is what they said (Megillah 18a): the Holy One, blessed be He, called Jacob 'El' — that is to say, ruling over Him, as it were. And this is what is said here: 'וְאַתֶּם תִּהְיוּ לִי מַמְלֶכֶת' — kings to Me, as it were; I decree a decree and the righteous one annuls it. And they merited this level by virtue of their being children of Israel, as the passage begins: 'כֹּה תֹאמַר לְבֵית יַעֲקֹב וְתַגֵּיד לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל' etc." "And we may interpret the verse 'וְאַתֶּם תִּהְיוּ לִי מַמְלֶכֶת כֹּהֲנִים' as: kings over the priests — for through the acceptance of the Torah, those who study it have superiority over the priests, for even a mamzer who is a Torah scholar takes precedence over a High Priest who is an ignoramus (Horiyyot 13a). And some say: 'אַתֶּם רְאִיתֶם אֵת אֲשֶׁר עָשִׂיתִי לְמִצְרָיִם' — that is, the plagues. For at the time when the plagues came from the earth — blood, frogs, and their like — then 'וָאֶשָּׂא אֶתְכֶם עַל כַּנְפֵי נְשָׁרִים,' so that you would not be harmed by them, as though I lifted you up from the earth. And afterward, when the plagues came from the heavens — fire and hail — then 'וָאָבִא אֶתְכֶם אֵלָי,' to lift you up above the array. And some say that He lifted them above the angels, who are called possessors of wings, and concerning this He said, 'וָאֶשָּׂא אֶתְכֶם עַל כַּנְפֵי נְשָׁרִים' — that is to say: I bore you to a level that is above the wings of eagles."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {2 "In the second reading, 'above the array' (מִן הַמַּעֲרָכָה) likely refers to the celestial array/order from which heavenly plagues descend; translated literally as the Hebrew is unusual in this context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:21:34.859803410Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}